Part 3 — JSON.parse() with a Reviver Function
The journal also demonstrates a JSON.parse() callback that removes a particular property when the key is "sem".     BDNS Journal
Step 6 — Create parsing2.html
<html>

<body>

<script type="text/javascript">

    var data = '{"name":"Swadesh","rollno":239715,"class":"TYIT","sem":5}';

    var jsonObj = JSON.parse(data, function(name, value) {

        if (name == "sem") {
            return undefined;
        }
        else {
            return value;
        }

    });

    for (key in jsonObj) {

        document.writeln(
            "<br/>" + key + " : " + jsonObj[key]
        );

    }

</script>

</body>

</html>

Output
name : Swadesh
rollno : 239715
class : TYIT

The sem field is not displayed because the reviver returns undefined when:
name == "sem"

The journal's output similarly shows the remaining fields without sem.     
